#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "## AT Records\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "The AT Protocol distributes user data across different nodes of your network, using the core building blocks of the web. The AT Protocol interconnects applications so that their backends share state, including user accounts and content, in individual data repositories. Many operations you'll perform when working with AT apps involve reading and writing these data repositories.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "For more context, see [ATProto for distributed systems engineers](/articles/atproto-for-distsys-engineers) and [The ATProto Ethos](/articles/atproto-ethos).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "## Prerequisites\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reading and writing AT data repositories requires an authenticated client. You can authenticate with [password authentication](/guides/sdk-auth) or [OAuth](/guides/oauth-patterns). The guides in this section assume you have already set up an authenticated client.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "## API Methods\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "After [authenticating](/guides/sdk-auth#creating-a-client), you'll be able to make other API requests.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<TabGroup defaultValue=\"ts\">\n"
"  <TabList>\n"
"    <Tab value=\"ts\">TypeScript</Tab>\n"
"    <Tab value=\"go\">Go</Tab>\n"
"  </TabList>\n"
"  <TabPanels>\n"
"    <TabPanel value=\"ts\">\n"
"    To make API requests, you'll need to specify a particular [Lexicon](/guides/lexicon). For example, Bluesky posts are available under the `app.bsky.feed.post` Lexicon.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "    Use `lex` to install the `app.bsky.feed.post` and `app.bsky.actor.profile` Lexicons as described in [Installing Lexicons](/guides/installing-lexicons):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:36
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```bash\n"
"    npm install -g @atproto/lex\n"
"    lex install app.bsky.feed.post app.bsky.actor.profile\n"
"    lex build\n"
"    ```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "    This will generate TypeScript types for the Lexicons in your project. You can use this `import` statement to access all installed Lexicons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```tsx\n"
"    import * as app from './lexicons/app.js'\n"
"    ```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    This will give you access to the API methods for your Lexicons. Now you are ready to start [making requests](/guides/reading-data)!\n"
"    </TabPanel>\n"
"    <TabPanel value=\"go\">\n"
"  ```go\n"
"  import (\n"
"    \"context\"\n"
"    appbsky \"github.com/bluesky-social/indigo/api/bsky\"\n"
"    comatproto \"github.com/bluesky-social/indigo/api/atproto\"\n"
"    lexutil \"github.com/bluesky-social/indigo/lex/util\"\n"
"    \"github.com/bluesky-social/indigo/xrpc\"\n"
"  )\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  ctx := context.Background()\n"
"  ```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/reads-and-writes/en.mdx:62
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Import the SDK libraries and go directly to [making requests](/guides/reading-data)!\n"
"    </TabPanel>\n"
"  </TabPanels>\n"
"</TabGroup>\n"
msgstr ""

